---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Full-screen Workshop 1 presentation" description="Visual, interactive presenter deck for Git, GitHub, VS Code, Copilot, and GitHub Pages.">
<div class="presentation-page container">
  <div class="presentation-intro no-print">
    <div><div class="eyebrow">MIS Club · Workshop 1 · presenter mode</div><h1>Build it. Version it. Ship it.</h1><p>This is the visual presentation workspace: diagrams, workflow demos, presenter notes, and quick-reference links are all inside the deck.</p></div>
    <button class="btn primary launch-fullscreen" data-fullscreen>Enter full screen ↗</button>
  </div>
  <div class="presentation-root" id="presentation-root">
    <header class="deck-topbar no-print">
      <a class="deck-brand" href={import.meta.env.BASE_URL}><span class="deck-mark">W</span><span>WICHITA STATE <small>MIS CLUB · WORKSHOP 1</small></span></a>
      <div class="deck-tools">
        <button class="deck-tool" data-toggle="resources" aria-haspopup="dialog">☷ Resources <kbd>R</kbd></button>
        <button class="deck-tool" data-toggle="notes">Presenter notes <kbd>N</kbd></button>
        <button class="deck-tool" data-fullscreen>⛶ Full screen <kbd>F</kbd></button>
      </div>
    </header>
    <div class="deck-stage" id="deck-stage" aria-live="polite">
      <section class="deck-slide cover-slide" data-title="Build it. Version it. Ship it." data-notes="Welcome students. Begin with the outcome: a professional portfolio website that they own and can continue improving. Today is about a repeatable workflow, not memorizing every command. Point out the resources drawer and keyboard shortcuts.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>SECURE SOFTWARE DEVELOPMENT</span><span>01 / 14</span></div>
        <div class="cover-content"><div class="cover-copy"><div class="yellow-rule"></div><p class="eyebrow light">WORKSHOP 01 — GIT &amp; GITHUB</p><h2>Build it.<br/><span>Version it.</span><br/>Ship it.</h2><p class="cover-dek">A real portfolio project. A professional development workflow. A website you can keep using.</p><div class="cover-pills"><span>VS CODE</span><span>GITHUB</span><span>COPILOT</span><span>GITHUB PAGES</span></div></div>
          <div class="cover-art" aria-label="A visual sequence from code to deployment"><div class="art-grid"></div><div class="art-card art-code"><span class="window-dots">● ● ●</span><span class="code-line long"></span><span class="code-line medium"></span><span class="code-line short"></span><span class="code-line long"></span><span class="code-line medium"></span></div><div class="art-arrow">→</div><div class="art-card art-live"><span class="live-dot"></span><small>LIVE PORTFOLIO</small><b>YOUR<br/>NEXT<br/>CHAPTER.</b><span class="live-line"></span><span class="live-line half"></span></div><div class="art-number">01<span> / BUILD</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>ONE PROJECT · FOUR WORKSHOPS</span><span>WICHITA STATE UNIVERSITY</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="Start with the outcome" data-notes="Show the three portfolio styles in the right pane. They are illustrative examples, not three separate codebases. Ask: what would you want a recruiter or interviewer to notice first? Emphasize that example content is fictional and students should only publish information they are comfortable making public.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>THE OUTCOME</span><span>02 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A PROJECT YOU CAN KEEP</p><h2>Not a throwaway exercise.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">By the end, your website is online, your code is in your repository, and you can explain how a change gets published.</p></div>
        <div class="outcome-grid"><div class="outcome-list"><article><b>01</b><div><h3>Your own repository</h3><p>Files, history, issues, branches, and reviewed changes.</p></div></article><article><b>02</b><div><h3>Your own portfolio</h3><p>Education, experience, skills, selected projects, and professional links.</p></div></article><article><b>03</b><div><h3>Your own live URL</h3><p>Published with GitHub Pages and ready to share.</p></div></article></div><div class="portfolio-triptych"><div class="mini-portfolio editorial"><span>PROFILE / 2026</span><b>Jordan<br/><i>Lee.</i></b><hr/><small>Business · Technology · People</small><div class="mini-lines"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div></div><div class="mini-portfolio developer"><span>~/PORTFOLIO</span><b>Hi, I'm Jordan<span>.</span></b><div class="mini-code">const work = [<br/> &nbsp; "projects",<br/> &nbsp; "learning"<br/>];</div><small>PYTHON · CLOUD · SECURITY</small></div><div class="mini-portfolio professional"><div class="mini-avatar">JL</div><b>Jordan Lee</b><small>MIS Student</small><hr/><span>EXPERIENCE</span><div class="mini-lines"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div></div></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>THREE VISUAL STYLES · ONE SHARED FOUNDATION</span><span>EXAMPLE PROFILES ARE FICTIONAL</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="Git, GitHub, and VS Code" data-notes="Use the diagram to separate three concepts that students often blend together. VS Code is the editor. Git tracks versions and branches on the computer. GitHub hosts the remote repository and adds collaboration features such as issues and pull requests. A commit can exist locally before it is pushed.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>THE MENTAL MODEL</span><span>03 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THREE TOOLS, DIFFERENT JOBS</p><h2>Git is not GitHub.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">The editor helps you change code. Git records history. GitHub hosts that history and makes collaboration possible.</p></div>
        <div class="concept-diagram">
          <div class="concept-card editor-card"><div class="concept-icon">⌘</div><span class="concept-label">YOU WORK HERE</span><h3>VS Code</h3><p>Edit files, preview the site, inspect diffs, and use Source Control.</p><div class="file-stack"><span>index.astro</span><span>global.css</span><span>resume.ts</span></div></div>
          <div class="diagram-connector"><span>edit / inspect</span><svg viewBox="0 0 100 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 20H88" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/><path d="M78 9L90 20 78 31" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/></svg></div>
          <div class="concept-card git-card"><div class="concept-icon git-icon">⑂</div><span class="concept-label">VERSION CONTROL</span><h3>Git</h3><p>Track changes over time and organize work in commits and branches.</p><div class="commit-stack"><span><i></i> a91c7f2 · Update bio</span><span><i></i> b8421a0 · Add projects</span><span><i></i> 3ef019a · Initial site</span></div></div>
          <div class="diagram-connector"><span>push / pull</span><svg viewBox="0 0 100 40" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M4 20H88" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/><path d="M78 9L90 20 78 31" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2.5" fill="none"/></svg></div>
          <div class="concept-card github-card"><div class="concept-icon github-icon">GH</div><span class="concept-label">HOST + COLLABORATE</span><h3>GitHub</h3><p>Remote repositories, issues, pull requests, review, and Actions.</p><div class="gh-badges"><span>Issues</span><span>Pull requests</span><span>Actions</span></div></div>
        </div><div class="diagram-caption"><span class="yellow-dot"></span>Think: <strong>edit locally → record history → share with GitHub.</strong></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>LOCAL CHANGES CAN EXIST BEFORE YOU PUSH</span><span>KEY CONCEPT</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="The Git change lifecycle" data-notes="Walk from left to right. Working directory: edits are not yet recorded. Staging area: choose exactly what belongs in the next commit. Local repository: the commit stores a snapshot in history. Remote repository: push shares commits with GitHub. VS Code provides a visual interface for these actions; students don't have to memorize every CLI command today.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>FOLLOW ONE CHANGE</span><span>04 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THE GIT LIFECYCLE</p><h2>Every change has a path.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">The staging area is the checkpoint between editing files and recording a commit.</p></div>
        <div class="lifecycle">
          <article class="life-step"><div class="life-number">1</div><div class="life-icon icon-file">▤</div><span class="life-state">EDIT</span><h3>Working directory</h3><p>Files have changed on your computer.</p><div class="life-sample"><span class="minus">−</span> Old bio<br/><span class="plus">+</span> New bio</div></article>
          <div class="life-arrow"><b>git add</b><svg viewBox="0 0 70 30"><path d="M2 15H59M49 5L60 15 49 25" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/></svg></div>
          <article class="life-step"><div class="life-number">2</div><div class="life-icon icon-stage">▣</div><span class="life-state">SELECT</span><h3>Staging area</h3><p>Pick the edits that belong together.</p><div class="life-sample staged"><span>✓</span> bio section<br/><span>✓</span> styles</div></article>
          <div class="life-arrow"><b>git commit</b><svg viewBox="0 0 70 30"><path d="M2 15H59M49 5L60 15 49 25" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/></svg></div>
          <article class="life-step"><div class="life-number">3</div><div class="life-icon icon-commit">●</div><span class="life-state">RECORD</span><h3>Local repository</h3><p>A commit creates a point in history.</p><div class="life-sample commit-hash"><b>4fa21c8</b><br/>Update bio</div></article>
          <div class="life-arrow"><b>git push</b><svg viewBox="0 0 70 30"><path d="M2 15H59M49 5L60 15 49 25" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" fill="none"/></svg></div>
          <article class="life-step remote-step"><div class="life-number">4</div><div class="life-icon icon-remote">GH</div><span class="life-state">SHARE</span><h3>GitHub remote</h3><p>Your commits are available on GitHub.</p><div class="life-sample remote-sample"><span class="green-check">✓</span> Pushed to origin</div></article>
        </div><div class="callout-bar"><b>Before you commit:</b> use <code>git status</code> and inspect the diff so you know what you're recording.</div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>WORKING TREE → STAGE → COMMIT → PUSH</span><span>YOU CONTROL WHAT GOES INTO A COMMIT</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="Branches and pull requests" data-notes="Explain branch as a named line of work, not a separate copy of the whole project that must be managed manually. The feature branch lets you try a focused improvement. The pull request is the discussion/review boundary before merge. In this workshop, each student can review their own PR against their issue's acceptance criteria; a partner review is a useful extension.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>CHANGE WITHOUT CHAOS</span><span>05 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">BRANCHES + PULL REQUESTS</p><h2>Keep new work reviewable.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">A branch isolates the change. A pull request makes the diff visible before it becomes part of main.</p></div>
        <div class="branch-board">
          <div class="branch-label main-label">main <small>published baseline</small></div>
          <div class="branch-lane main-lane"><span class="branch-node"></span><span class="branch-node"></span><span class="branch-node node-yellow"></span><span class="branch-node"></span></div>
          <div class="branch-line-join"></div>
          <div class="branch-label feature-label">feature/project-card <small>your change</small></div>
          <div class="branch-lane feature-lane"><span class="branch-node"></span><span class="branch-node node-yellow"></span><span class="branch-node"></span><span class="branch-merge">✓</span></div>
          <div class="branch-annotations"><div><b>1. Create branch</b><span>Start from main.</span></div><div><b>2. Commit changes</b><span>Make small, named checkpoints.</span></div><div><b>3. Open a PR</b><span>Review diff + criteria.</span></div><div><b>4. Merge</b><span>Approved change joins main.</span></div></div>
        </div>
        <div class="pr-example"><div class="pr-top"><span class="pr-green">✓ Open</span><b>Add featured project card</b><span class="pr-muted">#3</span></div><div class="pr-meta">feature/project-card → main · 2 files changed</div><div class="diff-lines"><div><span>−</span> <i class="diff-old"></i></div><div><span>+</span> <i class="diff-new long"></i></div><div><span>+</span> <i class="diff-new"></i></div></div><div class="pr-footer"><span>✓ Meets acceptance criteria</span><span>Ready to merge</span></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>ISSUE → BRANCH → PULL REQUEST → REVIEW → MERGE</span><span>SMALL DIFFS ARE EASIER TO REVIEW</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="Define done before code" data-notes="Show the issue card. Point out that acceptance criteria make a task checkable. Ask students how they would validate a project card before merging. Good criteria refer to visible behavior, links, layout, accessibility, and constraints—not vague outcomes like 'make it look better'.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>REQUIREMENTS BEFORE CODE</span><span>06 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">ISSUES + ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA</p><h2>Make “done” observable.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">A good issue creates a shared understanding of what the change is supposed to accomplish.</p></div>
        <div class="issue-layout"><div class="issue-card"><div class="issue-head"><span class="issue-open">● Open</span><span class="issue-id">Issue #3</span></div><h3>Add one featured project card</h3><p>Add a card to the projects section that highlights a project worth discussing in an interview.</p><div class="issue-section-label">ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA</div><label class="fake-check"><i>✓</i><span>The card includes a title and a concise summary.</span></label><label class="fake-check"><i>✓</i><span>A relevant link opens successfully.</span></label><label class="fake-check"><i>✓</i><span>The card fits on mobile without horizontal scrolling.</span></label><label class="fake-check"><i>✓</i><span>No new dependency is required.</span></label><div class="issue-labels"><span>enhancement</span><span>workshop-1</span></div></div><div class="criteria-side"><div class="criteria-mark">✓</div><h3>Test the result against the criteria.</h3><p>Not “the AI generated code.” Not “it looked okay on my laptop.”</p><div class="criteria-equation"><span>Requirement</span><b>+</b><span>Evidence</span><b>=</b><strong>Done</strong></div><div class="notice-lite">The pull request should explain how the change satisfies each criterion.</div></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>ISSUES CAPTURE THE WHY</span><span>ACCEPTANCE CRITERIA HELP REVIEW THE WHAT</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="Copilot: ask, inspect, verify" data-notes="Model this live in VS Code if time permits. Ask Copilot to explain the project structure first, then propose a small focused change. Inspect the diff, preview the site, and only then commit. Students are responsible for reviewing generated code. Do not paste secrets, passwords, private keys, or sensitive personal information into prompts.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>AI-ASSISTED DEVELOPMENT</span><span>07 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">COPILOT IS AN ASSISTANT, NOT AN AUTHORITY</p><h2>Ask. Inspect. Verify.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Keep a human in the loop at every step.</p></div>
        <div class="copilot-loop"><div class="copilot-step prompt-step"><span class="copilot-n">01</span><div class="copilot-icon">✎</div><h3>Describe</h3><p>State context, goal, and constraints.</p><div class="prompt-snippet">“Add one project card. No new dependencies.”</div></div><div class="loop-arrow">→</div><div class="copilot-step ai-step"><span class="copilot-n">02</span><div class="copilot-icon">✦</div><h3>Generate</h3><p>Copilot proposes code or an edit.</p><div class="prompt-snippet">Suggestion ≠ approved change</div></div><div class="loop-arrow">→</div><div class="copilot-step inspect-step"><span class="copilot-n">03</span><div class="copilot-icon">&lt;/&gt;</div><h3>Inspect</h3><p>Read the diff and ask questions.</p><div class="prompt-snippet">Which files changed? Why?</div></div><div class="loop-arrow">→</div><div class="copilot-step verify-step"><span class="copilot-n">04</span><div class="copilot-icon">✓</div><h3>Verify</h3><p>Preview, test, and check criteria.</p><div class="prompt-snippet">Works on desktop and mobile?</div></div></div>
        <div class="copilot-bottom"><span><b>THEN:</b> commit the change you understand.</span><span class="safety-badge">Never prompt with secrets or credentials.</span></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>AI CAN HELP BUILD IT</span><span>YOU ARE RESPONSIBLE FOR THE RESULT</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="Inside VS Code" data-notes="If students have VS Code open, mirror these elements on your machine: Explorer on the left, editor in the middle, browser preview, and Source Control to review/stage/commit changes. Copilot Chat can explain or propose focused changes. The interface is a conceptual classroom diagram, not a screenshot of a specific VS Code version.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>WHERE THE WORK HAPPENS</span><span>08 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">VS CODE WORKSPACE</p><h2>Four things to recognize.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">You don't need every feature. Find the files, make a change, inspect it, and preview it.</p></div>
        <div class="vscode-window"><div class="vs-titlebar"><span class="vs-logo">⌘</span><span>workshopsite — Visual Studio Code</span><span class="vs-title-spacer"></span><span>— □ ×</span></div><div class="vs-body"><aside class="vs-activity"><b>▦</b><b>⌕</b><b class="active">⑂</b><b>◇</b></aside><aside class="vs-explorer"><span>EXPLORER</span><b>⌄ WORKSHOP SITE</b><label>▸ .github</label><label class="selected">▾ src</label><label class="nested">▾ pages</label><label class="nested selected-file">▸ slides</label><label class="nested">▸ workshop</label><label>▸ styles</label><label>▤ package.json</label><label>▤ README.md</label><div class="vs-highlight">① Project files</div></aside><div class="vs-editor"><div class="vs-tab">index.astro <span>×</span></div><div class="vs-code"><span class="line-no">1</span><code><em>const</em> feature = {</code><br/><span class="line-no">2</span><code>&nbsp; title: <strong>'Campus Resource Finder'</strong>,</code><br/><span class="line-no">3</span><code>&nbsp; skills: [<strong>'UX'</strong>, <strong>'HTML'</strong>],</code><br/><span class="line-no">4</span><code>&nbsp; link: <strong>'https://github.com/...'</strong></code><br/><span class="line-no">5</span><code>};</code><div class="vs-highlight editor-highlight">② Edit + explain with Copilot</div></div><div class="vs-source"><b>⑂ SOURCE CONTROL</b><p>Changes <span class="vs-count">2</span></p><label>M index.astro</label><label>M global.css</label><div class="vs-highlight source-highlight">③ Inspect diff + commit</div></div></div><aside class="vs-chat"><div class="vs-chat-title">✦ Copilot Chat</div><p>Explain where project data is defined. Don't change files yet.</p><div class="vs-chat-answer">Portfolio content lives in the data file. The page component renders it.</div><div class="vs-chat-input">Ask a follow-up…</div><div class="vs-highlight chat-highlight">④ Ask focused questions</div></aside></div><div class="vs-statusbar"><span>main*</span><span>Astro · UTF-8</span><span>Ready to preview</span></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>CONCEPTUAL WORKSPACE DIAGRAM</span><span>YOUR VS CODE VIEW MAY LOOK A LITTLE DIFFERENT</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="Choose your portfolio design" data-notes="Let students choose quickly, not debate indefinitely. Each layout shares the same content model and project structure. Editorial: warm and narrative-led. Developer: dark and project-led. Professional: structured and easy to scan. Use the Designs page in the Resources drawer if you want to switch to a live preview during class; the links open in a new tab, so return to this presentation tab afterward.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>YOUR SITE, YOUR STYLE</span><span>09 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">THREE STARTING POINTS</p><h2>Choose a design. Own the content.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">All three options use the same content and workflow. Change the visual language, not the learning objectives.</p></div>
        <div class="design-cards"><article class="design-card editorial-card"><div class="design-meta">01 · EDITORIAL</div><div class="design-mock ed-mock"><small>PROFILE / 2026</small><b>Thoughtful.<br/><i>By design.</i></b><div class="ed-columns"><i></i><i></i></div><div class="ed-foot">WORK · EXPERIENCE · EDUCATION</div></div><h3>Story-led</h3><p>Warm neutrals, editorial typography, strong narrative.</p><span class="design-tag">Best for: broad professional profile</span></article><article class="design-card dev-card"><div class="design-meta">02 · DEVELOPER</div><div class="design-mock dev-mock"><small>~/PORTFOLIO</small><b>Build<span>.</span><br/>Create<span>_</span></b><div class="dev-projects"><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><div class="dev-foot">CODE · PROJECTS · SKILLS</div></div><h3>Project-led</h3><p>Dark palette, technical details, projects up front.</p><span class="design-tag">Best for: technical roles</span></article><article class="design-card professional-card"><div class="design-meta">03 · PROFESSIONAL</div><div class="design-mock pro-mock"><div class="pro-sim-side"><b>JL</b><i></i><i></i><i></i></div><div class="pro-sim-main"><b>Professional Profile</b><i></i><i></i><i></i><div class="pro-sim-box"></div><b>Experience</b><i></i><i></i></div></div><h3>Structured</h3><p>Clear hierarchy and a recruiter-friendly profile.</p><span class="design-tag">Best for: concise resume-style storytelling</span></article></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>DESIGN PREVIEWS LIVE INSIDE THE WORKSHOP SITE</span><span>OPEN RESOURCES → PORTFOLIO DESIGNS</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="From GitHub to Pages" data-notes="Explain that the published site is generated from the project source. With this course starter, GitHub Actions installs dependencies, runs Astro's static build, uploads the dist folder as a Pages artifact, and deploys it. The green deployment check tells us the workflow finished; the real proof is opening the actual URL and checking links/assets there.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>PUBLISH THE WEBSITE</span><span>10 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">GITHUB PAGES + ACTIONS</p><h2>Push the source. Publish the build.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Astro converts the project into static files. GitHub Actions publishes those generated files to Pages.</p></div>
        <div class="deploy-flow"><article class="deploy-box local-box"><div class="deploy-icon">⌨</div><span>01 · LOCAL</span><h3>VS Code + Git</h3><p>Change files, commit, and push to main.</p><div class="deploy-mini-files"><i>index.astro</i><i>global.css</i><i>package.json</i></div></article><div class="deploy-arrow"><b>push</b><span>→</span></div><article class="deploy-box actions-box"><div class="deploy-icon">⚙</div><span>02 · AUTOMATE</span><h3>GitHub Actions</h3><p>Install → build → upload artifact.</p><div class="action-steps"><i>✓ Checkout</i><i>✓ npm install</i><i>✓ astro build</i><i>✓ Upload dist</i></div></article><div class="deploy-arrow"><b>deploy</b><span>→</span></div><article class="deploy-box pages-box"><div class="deploy-icon">↗</div><span>03 · LIVE</span><h3>GitHub Pages</h3><p>Serves the published static site.</p><div class="fake-url"><span class="live-dot"></span> username.github.io/repo/</div></article></div>
        <div class="deploy-footer"><div><b>Source code</b><span>HTML / CSS / Astro</span></div><div class="deploy-down">▼</div><div><b>Build output</b><span>Static files in <code>dist/</code></span></div><div class="deploy-down">▼</div><div><b>Published site</b><span>A URL others can open</span></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>CHECK THE ACTIONS RUN AND TEST THE REAL URL</span><span>LOCAL PREVIEW ≠ DEPLOYED SITE</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="Where Pages is configured" data-notes="The exact GitHub UI may vary. In the repository, open Settings → Pages. For this workshop starter, the source is GitHub Actions, not a branch/folder deployment. Open the Actions tab to inspect the workflow. The repository's project Pages URL usually includes the repository name as a path, so the Astro base path matters.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>SHOW THE PUBLISHING CONFIGURATION</span><span>11 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">GITHUB SETTINGS → PAGES</p><h2>Know where to look.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">A conceptual UI walkthrough: students should find the actual Settings and Actions pages in their own repository.</p></div>
        <div class="settings-mock"><div class="settings-sidebar"><b>Repository settings</b><span>General</span><span>Collaborators</span><span>Pages</span><span>Environments</span><span>Secrets and variables</span></div><div class="settings-content"><div class="settings-breadcrumb">Settings <span>›</span> Pages</div><h3>Build and deployment</h3><p>Choose how your site is built and published.</p><div class="settings-field"><label>Source</label><div class="settings-select"><span class="settings-check">✓</span> GitHub Actions <span>⌄</span></div></div><div class="settings-workflow"><div><span class="settings-check">✓</span><div><b>Build and deploy workshop site</b><small>Workflow: .github/workflows/deploy.yml</small></div></div><span class="workflow-status">SUCCESS</span></div><div class="settings-live"><span class="settings-check">✓</span><div><b>Your site is live</b><small>https://username.github.io/repository/</small></div><span>↗</span></div></div><div class="settings-callout">① Select<br/><b>GitHub Actions</b><br/><br/>② Check the latest workflow run<br/><br/>③ Open the published URL</div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>ILLUSTRATIVE UI — NOT A SCREENSHOT</span><span>SETTINGS → PAGES · ACTIONS TAB</span></div>
      </section>

      <section class="deck-slide" data-title="When deployment fails" data-notes="Model the order of troubleshooting: open the most recent Actions run, identify which step failed, read the first actionable error, fix the smallest cause, push again, and verify the live URL. Common first checks are dependency installation, build errors, Pages source setting, and repository base path. Don't randomly change configuration without a hypothesis.">
        <div class="slide-overline"><span>DEBUG WITH EVIDENCE</span><span>12 / 14</span></div><div class="slide-heading"><p class="eyebrow">A REPEATABLE TROUBLESHOOTING PATH</p><h2>When the page is broken, follow the evidence.</h2><p class="slide-subtitle">Treat an error as a clue. Find the failing step before changing code.</p></div>
        <div class="debug-flow"><article><b>1</b><h3>Open Actions</h3><p>Find the latest run for your commit.</p></article><div>→</div><article><b>2</b><h3>Find the failed step</h3><p>Install? Build? Upload? Deploy?</p></article><div>→</div><article><b>3</b><h3>Read the error</h3><p>Find the first actionable line.</p></article><div>→</div><article><b>4</b><h3>Fix + verify</h3><p>Push, wait, open the real URL.</p></article></div>
        <div class="log-window"><div class="log-top"><span>● ● ●</span><b>GitHub Actions · Build and deploy</b><span>step logs</span></div><div class="log-row good"><span>✓</span><b>Checkout repository</b><small>success</small></div><div class="log-row good"><span>✓</span><b>Install dependencies</b><small>success</small></div><div class="log-row error"><span>×</span><b>Build static site</b><small>failed · inspect output</small></div><div class="log-error">Error: A file failed to compile. Review the first error line, fix the referenced file, and run the build again.</div><div class="log-row muted-row"><span>○</span><b>Upload Pages artifact</b><small>skipped because build failed</small></div></div>
        <div class="slide-bottom"><span>READ THE LOG BEFORE YOU CHANGE THE CONFIGURATION</span><span>SMALLEST SAFE FIX</span></div>
      </section>

